Facebook ads for chiropractors have a reputation problem. Ask ten practice owners and half will tell you they tried them and "they don't work". When we look at what happened, the ads usually did their job. People clicked. People even filled in the form. Then nobody called them for a day, and the money was gone.

So this guide starts earlier than most. Before chiropractic Facebook ads are worth a dollar, two things need to be true in your practice. After that, the ads themselves are the simple part.

## First, ask the patients you already have

The cheapest new appointment is a past patient who has not been in for a while. They already trust you. They do not need an ad. They need a reason and a nudge.

One of our clients is a chiropractic practice in Tampa. It had eleven years of patients in its software, and nobody had been contacted after their last visit. Growth had stalled at referrals. We did not run ads. We sent a reactivation campaign to 640 patients who had not been seen in more than twelve months, written in the doctor's own voice, with a review request after every completed visit.

In the first month, 31 of them rebooked, and the practice gained 19 new Google reviews. Ad spend: zero. The owner put it this way:

> Patients we assumed had moved away. They just hadn't been asked.

If you have years of past patients and no system for contacting them, do this before you advertise. It fills the first gaps in your schedule and pays for the ads you run next.

## Second, reply in minutes, not tomorrow

An ad lead is not a referral. A person who taps an ad on their phone at 9 pm is curious, not committed. If you call them at 10 am the next day, they have forgotten, or they have booked with the practice that answered.

We do not have a chiropractic ads case study to show you yet, so here are two honest ones from other specialties, where the lesson is the same.

A med spa in Las Vegas had a strong Instagram following and a weak calendar. Messages were answered the next day, if at all. We ran Instagram and Meta campaigns to one consult offer, and set up replies to every message and form within minutes. That quarter it booked 62 consults, and 54 showed. Same followers, same budget. In the owner's words, the difference was somebody answering at 9 pm.

A physical therapy practice in Denver saw the same thing with Google campaigns and a callback within four minutes on every form. The owner's verdict: the four-minute callbacks did more than the new ads did.

Before you launch, send an enquiry to your own practice in the evening and time the reply. That number tells you more about how your ads will perform than anything in the ad account.

## What a good chiropractic Facebook ad looks like

Once those two things are in place, keep the campaign plain.

- **One offer.** A new-patient visit with a clear description of what is included. Not a list of everything you treat.
- **One audience.** People who live or work within a realistic drive of the practice. You do not need clever targeting; you need the right area.
- **One page.** Send the click to a page built for that offer, with the offer repeated at the top and a way to book or ask for a time. Not your homepage.
- **Real photos or video.** You, your team, your rooms. Patients are choosing a person, and stock images of spines do not help them choose.

## What Meta will not let you say

Healthcare ads are held to stricter rules, and chiropractic ads are rejected more often than most for one reason: the wording.

- **Do not tell the reader they have a condition.** Meta does not allow ads that imply you know something personal about someone, including their health. "Do you suffer from sciatica?" is a problem. "Sciatica care in Tampa, new patients welcome" describes what you offer and is far safer.
- **Do not promise a cure or a guaranteed result.** Describe the visit and what you help with.
- **Be careful with before-and-after images.** Pictures that imply a dramatic or unlikely result are not accepted.

Meta updates these rules, and it has been tightening how health advertisers may use tracking. Read the current advertising policy before each new campaign, and expect an occasional rejection even when you follow it. Edit and resubmit; do not try to trick the review.

## Measure visits, not clicks

An agency report full of clicks, impressions and "leads" tells you almost nothing. Ask for three numbers, every week:

1. How many people booked a first visit from the ads
2. How many of them showed up
3. What each of those visits cost

If your agency or your software cannot tell you those three, fix the measurement before you raise the budget. It is the only way to know whether the ads or the follow-up need work.

## A short checklist before you spend

- Past patients are being contacted, on a schedule
- Every enquiry gets a reply within minutes, evenings included
- The ad leads to a page built for the offer, with a way to book
- The ad copy describes what you offer, not what the reader suffers from
- You can count booked visits and visits that showed

Tick all five and Facebook ads become a way to grow a practice that already converts. Skip them and you are paying to send people to a closed door.

